      "The line under a card's answer is now just the dates. It had grown into a second summary of the card — on some cards three sentences of it — under a label that only repeated what kind of thing the answer was. Every one of the 112 cards has been rewritten to a short list you can learn along with the answer: when it lived, when it was in use, when it was found, when it was named. A card with no obvious date now shows nothing there at all. Fifteen cards were also sorting into the wrong place in their deck, because the old paragraph mentioned the year a site was dug and the study page read that as the card's own date — Atapuerca, whose caves hold 1.4 million years, was filed under 1978.",
      "The Ancient Greece collection has its first ten cards, all in the Bronze Age Aegean deck — the Aegean and Cycladic Bronze Age, the islanders who crewed fifty-oar ships between barren rocks, Crete before and during the age of the palaces, what a Minoan palace was actually for, Knossos itself and the room with the throne in it, and the man who gave the Minoans their name. Each is written about the people who lived it rather than about the digs that found it, and each lists the works behind it as every card does. The collection can now be studied rather than sitting empty.",
      "Cards and glossary entries corrected: the Cave of Altamira's date line said a rockfall closed the cave about 13,000 years ago, which the works behind the card do not support \u2014 the claim was taken out of the card's background a while back and had been left standing on the date line. The card on the Minoan palace gave the building dates of the palace at Knossos as though every Minoan palace shared them; it now gives the two palace periods instead, and says that these buildings were not all founded or abandoned together \u2014 the one at Kommos stood for fifty years at most. And where a card or a term translates a name, the sense it gives is now in quotation marks, as it should always have been: a Latin word for 'handy', a cave whose name means 'hollow rock'. Twenty-four cards and five terms were put right.",
      "You can now read the site in metric or in imperial, and you see only the one you pick \u2014 there is a Measurements setting for it under Appearance. Every distance, height, area, weight and temperature on every card and in every glossary entry was first written out in both, including the ones that spell the number out in words, and three figures that were the wrong way round were turned around; the setting then shows you kilometres or miles, and never both at once. Scientific figures such as cranial capacities keep their own units either way.",
      "Light and dark can now follow your device. There is a Match my device switch in Settings, on from the first time you open Folio, so the site is dark when your phone or laptop is and changes with it through the day; turning Night mode on or off by hand takes the decision back.",
      "Folio moves more smoothly. Pages now fade into one another instead of cutting, the blocks on a page arrive in order rather than all at once, dialogs rise into view, and the quieter buttons acknowledge a press. All of it stops if your device asks for reduced motion.",
      "The Ordered / Random switch has left the corner of the daily review. Hold the review down \u2014 the same way you hold a deck under it \u2014 and it is there among the options, with the current one ticked.",
